  "confound_note": "cpgs_covered_only_auc_loo is the baseline: if the number of CpGs with coverage alone separates the classes as well as the beta summary does, then the beta summary is not adding anything over library complexity, and the two must be compared before the channel is claimed.",
  "reproducibility_note": "The headline numbers here are reproducible (decoded-beta AUC, the covered-CpG and observations-per-CpG baselines, the depth-matched pair, and the per-CpG median difference of exactly 0.0 over 103,863 CpGs with permutation p = 1.0 -- all identical across repeated runs). The shared-locus (common_cpg_set) AUC is NOT: FinaleMe's HMM training is not deterministic. On byte-identical decoder inputs the trained model differs between runs, which flips 16-20 of ~51,000 CpG calls per sample, and the shared-locus AUC moves with it (0.803-0.829 observed over five runs). Read that row as a range, not a value. The scoring code itself is deterministic given the decoded files: the shared loci are read in sorted order, so the float summation does not depend on PYTHONHASHSEED.",
  "what_the_channel_actually_computes": "The decoder emits HARD 0/1 state assignments, not fractional beta: ~91% of the covered loci come back exactly 1.0 and the rest exactly 0.0, with nothing in between. So the per-sample summary is literally the FRACTION OF COVERED LOCI CALLED METHYLATED, over a locus set that differs between samples (~10k covered loci for the colorectal cancers, ~50k for the transplant controls). The per-CpG test shows the state assignment is identical between the arms at the same locus (median difference exactly 0), so the sample-level separation is coverage and composition, not methylation.",
  "honest_framing": "The HMM was trained once across the cohort and each sample was decoded separately, so every beta value is derived from that sample's own fragments with a shared model. The score above is a leave-one-out AUC on a four-number beta summary over a cohort of tens of samples: it is a feasibility measurement of the channel, not a validated feature. It will not survive a leave-one-study-out check, it uses no external cohort, and the GNN that would consume it is still untrained. It also has n=31 at most, so the leave-one-out AUC is unstable; read it next to the CpG-coverage baseline in the same artifact. CONFOUND TO CHECK BEFORE BELIEVING IT: the number of CpGs with coverage differs between samples (library complexity and depth), and the beta summary is not depth-normalised -- a difference in the summary can therefore reflect depth rather than biology. The per-sample n_cpgs column is the thing to look at first."
